{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:1.1", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "1.1", "chapter": "1", "verse": "1"}, "paraphrase": "Śrāvakas and middle buddhas (pratyekabuddhas) are born from the Lord of Sages; Buddhas are born from bodhisattvas; and compassion, non-dual understanding and the awakening mind are the causes of the Victors' children.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "causal", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge", "devotion"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["karma-liberation", "teacher-transmission"], "terms": ["trm:karuna", "trm:bodhicitta"], "concepts": ["cpt:bodhicitta", "cpt:mahakaruna"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:1.2", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "1.2", "chapter": "1", "verse": "2"}, "paraphrase": "Because compassion alone is the seed of the Victors' abundant harvest, the water that makes it grow and the ripening that lets it be long enjoyed, I praise compassion first of all.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion", "action"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "concepts": ["cpt:mahakaruna"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:1.3-4", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "1.3-4", "chapter": "1", "verse": "3-4"}, "paraphrase": "I bow to that compassion for beings who, first thinking 'I' of a self, then attach to things as 'mine', and are helpless like a bucket turning in a well; and to the compassion that sees beings as fleeting and as empty of own-nature, like the moon reflected in rippling water.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion", "knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ethics"], "concepts": ["cpt:three-kinds-of-compassion"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.120", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.120", "chapter": "6", "verse": "120"}, "paraphrase": "Seeing with wisdom that all afflictions and faults arise from the view of the transitory collection, and understanding that its object is the self, the yogin refutes the self.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge", "meditation"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice", "consciousness-mind"], "terms": ["trm:satkayadrsti"], "obstacles": ["obs:satkayadrsti"], "practices": ["prc:sevenfold-reasoning"], "disputes": ["dsp:is-there-a-self"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.151-160", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.151-160", "chapter": "6", "verse": "151-160"}, "paraphrase": "The chariot is not other than its parts, nor identical with them, nor possessing them; it is not in the parts nor are they in it; it is not the mere collection of the parts nor their shape. Yet it is designated in dependence on its parts; so too the self in dependence on the aggregates.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge", "meditation"], "stage": "intermediate"}, "types": ["practice", "ultimate"], "concepts": ["cpt:sevenfold-chariot-analysis", "cpt:self-as-dependent-designation", "cpt:upadaya-prajnapti"], "practices": ["prc:sevenfold-reasoning"], "disputes": ["dsp:is-there-a-self"], "notes": "Range of the sevenfold analysis given from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.179-223", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.179-223", "chapter": "6", "verse": "179-223"}, "paraphrase": "The emptinesses: emptiness taught at length as sixteen — of the inner, the outer, both, emptiness itself, the great, the ultimate, the conditioned, the unconditioned, what is beyond extremes, the beginningless and endless, what is not discarded, the nature, all dharmas, characteristics, the unapprehendable, and the non-entity nature — and in brief as four.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["ultimate"], "concepts": ["cpt:twenty-emptinesses", "cpt:emptiness-of-emptiness"], "notes": "Range and list order from memory; low confidence.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.23", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.23", "chapter": "6", "verse": "23"}, "paraphrase": "All things bear two natures, obtained by correct seeing and by false seeing; the object of correct seeing is reality, and that of false seeing is said to be conventional truth.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ultimate"], "concepts": ["cpt:two-truths"], "terms": ["trm:samvrti-satya", "trm:paramartha-satya"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.28", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.28", "chapter": "6", "verse": "28"}, "paraphrase": "Because delusion obscures the nature (of things) it is called 'concealer' (saṃvṛti); what it fabricates appears as true; the Sage called this the conventional truth; fabricated things are conventional.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ultimate"], "terms": ["trm:samvrti-satya", "trm:avidya"], "concepts": ["cpt:two-truths"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.4-5", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.4-5", "chapter": "6", "verse": "4-5"}, "paraphrase": "Even an ordinary person who, on hearing of emptiness, feels great joy again and again, whose eyes fill with tears and whose body hair stands on end, has the seed of the fully awakened mind; such a one is a vessel for this teaching, and the ultimate truth should be taught to them.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "experiential", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "beginner"}, "types": ["powers-experiences", "teacher-transmission"], "concepts": ["cpt:qualified-student"], "notes": "Verse numbers from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.45-97", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.45-97", "chapter": "6", "verse": "45-97"}, "paraphrase": "Refutation of mind-only: consciousness does not exist without an object any more than an object without consciousness; the dream example does not prove a real mind; reflexive awareness is not established; the sūtra's 'the three worlds are mind only' teaches that mind, not a creator, is the principal cause, and statements denying external objects are of provisional meaning.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute", "consciousness-mind"], "disputes": ["dsp:madhyamaka-yogacara", "dsp:svasamvedana"], "concepts": ["cpt:madhyamaka-critique-of-yogacara", "cpt:svasamvedana-critique"], "notes": "Range-level summary of a long section; exact verse boundaries from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.8", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.8", "chapter": "6", "verse": "8"}, "paraphrase": "(Things) do not arise from themselves; how could they arise from another? Not from both; how could they arise without a cause?", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "apophatic", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["ultimate"], "concepts": ["cpt:non-arising-four-alternatives"], "cross_refs": ["tea:mulamadhyamakakarika:1.1"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakavatara:6.80", "source": "src:madhyamakavatara", "location": {"ref": "6.80", "chapter": "6", "verse": "80"}, "paraphrase": "Conventional truth is the means and ultimate truth is what arises from the means; whoever does not know the distinction of the two truths enters wrong paths through false conceptions.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ultimate", "practice"], "concepts": ["cpt:two-truths"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:candrakirti"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
